---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Cite from '~/components/Cite.astro';
import RepresentationMap from '~/islands/RepresentationMap';
import raw from '~/data/representation/map.json';
import { atomCount, pairOf, type RepresentationMap as Data } from '~/lib/representation';

const map = raw as unknown as Data;
const nEnv = map.environments.length;
const nStruct = map.structures.length;
const nAtoms = atomCount(map);
const ms = pairOf(map, 'medium', 'small')!;
const mr = pairOf(map, 'medium', 'random')!;
const sr = pairOf(map, 'small', 'random')!;
const small = map.models.find((m) => m.id === 'small')!;
const random = map.models.find((m) => m.id === 'random')!;
const f2 = (x: number) => x.toFixed(2);
const procr = (m: typeof small) => Object.entries(m.anchored).map(([k, p]) => ({ k, v: p.procrustes ?? 0 }));
const range = (m: typeof small) => {
  const v = procr(m).map((x) => x.v);
  return `${f2(globalThis.Math.min(...v))}–${f2(globalThis.Math.max(...v))}`;
};
const kinds = ['crystal', 'surface', 'defect', 'molecule'].map((k) => ({ k, n: map.structures.filter((s) => s.kind === k).length }));
const ckMed = map.source.checkpoints.medium!;
const ckSmall = map.source.checkpoints.small!;

const assumptions = [
  { term: 'Reference set', value: `${nStruct} structures built with ASE: ${kinds.map((x) => `${x.n} ${x.k}${x.n === 1 ? '' : 's'}`).join(', ')}. Crystals are ideal, rattled (σ = 0.05 and 0.15 Å) and 5% compressed; lattice constants are inputs, not results. ${nAtoms} atoms, of which ${nEnv} have distinct environments within 6 Å; identical ones are one point.` },
  { term: 'Features', value: 'The invariant (0e) node features after each of the two interactions, concatenated: 256 numbers per atom for every model. For the medium model the l = 1 channels of the first layer are left out.' },
  { term: 'Models', value: 'MACE-MP-0a medium and small (MIT; both trained on MPtrj), and the medium architecture with random weights from torch.manual_seed(0). Checkpoint tensors read without executing pickled code; SHA-256 recorded in the data file.' },
  { term: 'Maps', value: 'PCA to two components (no UMAP: it distorts global distances). Anchored maps: cosine similarity to K anchor environments chosen round-robin over elements with a fixed seed, the same anchors for every model, then PCA and an orthogonal Procrustes rotation onto the medium model’s map.' },
  { term: 'Not shown', value: 'Other architectures (Orb, SevenNet, CHGNet), your own structure (needs live inference), and which training data covers which region.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Representation Map"
  description="Real MACE latent features of a reference set of atomic environments, precomputed on CPU: linked 2D maps for two trained checkpoints and an untrained control, the anchor-based relative representation, agreement metrics, and a 3D view of every environment."
>
  <article class="page-grid model-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/models/">Models</a> · Representation Map</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">Representation Map</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Inside every message-passing potential, each atom ends as a vector of learned numbers from which its energy is read. Two models
        trained on the same data learn different coordinates for those vectors, so they cannot be compared directly; a recent study
        compares them through their similarity to shared reference environments and finds a common geometry across seven foundation models
        <Cite id="li2026platonic" locator="Abstract; Figs. 1, 3" />. This page applies that method, on CPU, to the features of {nEnv} atomic
        environments from two MACE checkpoints and from the same architecture left untrained.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="what-h">
      <h2 id="what-h" class="h-section">What is being compared</h2>
      <p>
        MACE builds each atom’s features in two interactions of 6 Å each; the readout turns the invariant channels into the site energy
        (<a href="/models/mace/">MACE page</a>, <a href="/models/x-ray/">Architecture X-ray</a>). We take those invariant channels after each
        interaction, 256 numbers per atom, from MACE-MP-0a medium and small. Both were trained on MPtrj
        <Cite id="batatia2023foundation" />; they differ in that medium also carries l = 1 equivariant channels between layers. Raw features
        of different models live in unrelated coordinates: Li and Walsh found this even for MACE-MP-0 variants with the same data and
        objective <Cite id="li2026platonic" locator="Results; Fig. 1" />. Their remedy re-expresses every feature by its cosine similarity to
        K anchor environments, the same environments in every model <Cite id="li2026platonic" locator="Eqs. (1)–(2)" />:
      </p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`\mathbf z_i = \big[\cos(\mathbf e_i, \mathbf a_1),\ \dots,\ \cos(\mathbf e_i, \mathbf a_K)\big]^{\mathsf T}, \qquad \cos(\mathbf e, \mathbf a) = \frac{\mathbf e^{\mathsf T}\mathbf a}{\lVert\mathbf e\rVert\,\lVert\mathbf a\rVert}`} />
      <p>
        The new coordinates no longer depend on how a model happens to orient or scale its latent space, only on which environments it finds
        alike. To compare whole spaces without any anchors we also use linear centred kernel alignment, which is unchanged by rotating or
        scaling either space and equals 1 for identical geometry, and the overlap of each environment’s ten nearest neighbours:
      </p>
      <Math display number="2" tex={String.raw`\mathrm{CKA}(X, Y) = \frac{\lVert Y_c^{\mathsf T} X_c\rVert_F^2}{\lVert X_c^{\mathsf T} X_c\rVert_F\, \lVert Y_c^{\mathsf T} Y_c\rVert_F}, \qquad \mathrm{mkNN} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_i \frac{\lvert \mathcal N_k^X(i) \cap \mathcal N_k^Y(i)\rvert}{k}`} />
      <p class="small">
        X<sub>c</sub>, Y<sub>c</sub>: the feature matrices (atoms × channels) with each column’s mean removed. Both quantities are checked on
        synthetic data in <code>pipeline/tests/test_representation.py</code> (CKA = 1 under rotation and scaling, mkNN = 1 for a rotated copy).
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Three models, one set of environments" labels={['precomputed']} id="fig-map" framed={false}>
        <RepresentationMap client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Each point is one atomic environment, placed by PCA of one model’s features. Raw maps are in each model’s own coordinates; anchored
          maps use Eq. (1) and are rotated onto the medium map, so they can be compared by eye. Select an environment to see it in 3D and to see
          which environments each model puts nearest to it.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={assumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Features from CPU forward passes of <code>{ckMed.name}</code> (SHA-256 {ckMed.sha256.slice(0, 12)}…) and <code>{ckSmall.name}</code> ({ckSmall.sha256.slice(0, 12)}…) by <code>pipeline/atlas_pipeline/representation.py</code>; method from <Cite id="li2026platonic" locator="Eqs. (1)–(2)" />. Analysis functions tested in <code>pipeline/tests/test_representation.py</code>, the data file in <code>src/lib/representation.test.ts</code>.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="read-h">
      <h2 id="read-h" class="h-section">What the map shows, and what it does not</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Trained features agree more with each other than with an untrained network.</strong> The linear CKA between medium and
        small is {f2(ms.cka)}; between either and the random network it is {f2(mr.cka)} and {f2(sr.cka)}. After the anchor transform the
        small model’s map lands on the medium map with a Procrustes similarity of {range(small)} over K = 3 to 100; the untrained model’s with
        {range(random)}. This matches the direction of Li and Walsh’s result on seven models and 282,847 environments, where an untrained MACE
        showed no chemical organisation <Cite id="li2026platonic" locator="Fig. 3b,c" />, but with two checkpoints of one family and {nEnv}
        environments it is a demonstration of the method, not a measurement of convergence. Look at the untrained model’s anchored
        maps: its environments pile up in tight knots, each at the anchor from its own structure, with nothing arranging the knots by
        chemistry; the trained models spread the same environments into families of elements and bonding.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Nearest-neighbour overlap is high for every pair, the untrained one included</strong> ({f2(ms.mknn_raw)}, {f2(mr.mknn_raw)}
        and {f2(sr.mknn_raw)} in raw space). Our reference set is built from rattled and compressed copies of each crystal, and any network
        that keeps element and geometry information, trained or not, puts those copies next to each other. On this set the metric rewards how
        the set was made. Li and Walsh, on a broad set of real crystals, report the opposite pattern: global alignment high, local
        neighbourhood agreement low <Cite id="li2026platonic" locator="Fig. 4a,b" />. A reference set has to be chosen for the question it is
        meant to answer.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="caveat" title="Architecture ≠ checkpoint ≠ dataset">
        <p>
          Medium and small share an architecture family and a training set, so their agreement says nothing about architectures trained on
          other data. The published comparison found the largest distances between OMat24-trained and MPtrj-trained models and between
          equivariant and non-equivariant ones <Cite id="li2026platonic" locator="Fig. 4c" />. Distances in these maps are distances between
          learned features, not energy differences, and a point far from the training data is not necessarily predicted badly, nor one
          near it predicted well.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="next-h">
      <h2 id="next-h" class="h-section">Extending the map</h2>
      <p>
        The pipeline runs on CPU in about a minute for this set. Adding models means adding feature extractors: the
        <a href="/observatory/">Observatory</a> already runs CHGNet, SevenNet-0 and Orb v2 on CPU in separate environments, and the same
        structures could be passed through them. Placing a visitor’s own structure on the map needs a forward pass at request time, which the
        Atlas does not run without a CPU inference service. Colouring by training-set coverage needs the training structures’ features, which
        is a larger computation than this page’s.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="res-h">
      <h2 id="res-h" class="h-section">Resources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The relative (“Platonic”) representation of foundation MLIPs: <Cite id="li2026platonic" />.</li>
        <li>MACE-MP-0 and its checkpoints: <Cite id="batatia2023foundation" />; <a href="https://github.com/ACEsuit/mace-mp">ACEsuit/mace-mp</a> (MIT).</li>
        <li>Data: <code>atlas/src/data/representation/map.json</code> (structures, environments, 2D maps, neighbour lists and agreement metrics).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .model-page {
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
  .head .eyebrow a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .head h1 {
    margin-block: 0.6rem 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 6vw, 4.6rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .lede {
    font: 1.1875rem/1.6 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
    max-width: 46rem;
  }
  .block a {
    color: var(--atlas-arch);
  }
  .block {
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font: 1.0625rem/1.7 var(--font-serif);
  }
  .block p {
    margin-block: 0 1rem;
  }
  .fig-wrap {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .small {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
  }
</style>
